177
00:21:08,879 --> 00:21:10,440
This little material here.

178
00:21:11,060 --> 00:21:15,839
The fact is that if it would be distributed in the worst way

179
00:21:15,939 --> 00:21:24,200
through an explosion, anybody outside the immediate range of the explosion

180
00:21:24,300 --> 00:21:27,859
would be probably unharmed.

181
00:21:28,780 --> 00:21:31,920
The exaggeration is not a million fold.

182
00:21:32,020 --> 00:21:35,039
It is more than a billionfold.

183
00:21:36,139 --> 00:21:41,900
We must know the truth, the actual danger of plutonium which exists,

184
00:21:42,840 --> 00:21:49,060
but which is not great, and one should know the precise truth about it.

185
00:21:51,260 --> 00:21:59,600
I am now sitting at the control panel of one of the Oak Ridge reactors.

186
00:21:59,700 --> 00:22:10,740
In a few years from now, there will be a similar control panel for the fast breeder.

187
00:22:10,840 --> 00:22:29,819
I think I can say that there is no other reactor whose safety has been so thoroughly, so reliably analyzed as that of the fast breeder.

188
00:22:30,820 --> 00:22:39,240
Still, it is predictable that opponents of the fast breeder will raise objections.

189
00:22:40,840 --> 00:22:44,960
There is not as much experience with it

190
00:22:45,060 --> 00:22:48,599
as with the reactors which we are running now.

191
00:22:48,919 --> 00:22:49,720
Of course not.

192
00:22:51,820 --> 00:22:55,140
The fast breeder contains sodium

193
00:22:55,240 --> 00:22:58,220
in contact with water.

194
00:22:59,199 --> 00:23:01,299
It gives a violent reaction.

195
00:23:02,500 --> 00:23:04,460
There won't be any water there.

196
00:23:04,560 --> 00:23:20,000
still there is in the reactor a lot of active material if something should go wrong

197
00:23:20,520 --> 00:23:27,740
even a portion of this active material could become critical again

198
00:23:27,840 --> 00:23:32,960
if it got surrounded by water

199
00:23:33,060 --> 00:23:38,859
or by some other hydrogen substance there will be criticism

200
00:23:38,959 --> 00:23:42,819
and this has been

201
00:23:42,919 --> 00:23:47,500
dealt with and will be dealt with still

202
00:23:47,600 --> 00:23:51,000
to quiet all worries

203
00:23:51,100 --> 00:23:54,579
there might be a radical remedy

204
00:23:54,679 --> 00:23:58,299
future reactors,

205
00:23:58,840 --> 00:24:03,259
not the one we are building now, but future reactors

206
00:24:03,359 --> 00:24:08,559
might be designed for use underground

207
00:24:08,659 --> 00:24:14,199
deep enough, a couple of hundred feet,

208
00:24:15,139 --> 00:24:18,919
so that we know that no matter what happens,

209
00:24:19,779 --> 00:24:22,039
no radioactivity can come to the surface.

210
00:24:22,139 --> 00:24:31,159
We have lots of experience about that by having performed many explosions, nuclear explosions in Nevada.

211
00:24:31,939 --> 00:24:34,799
So we know very, very much about containment.

212
00:24:36,219 --> 00:24:38,879
I am not recommending that this be done.

213
00:24:39,959 --> 00:24:42,299
I am recommending that this be studied.

214
00:24:42,399 --> 00:24:54,299
And if the study shows that in this way, if the reactors are already properly designed,

215
00:24:54,399 --> 00:25:02,299
an underground reactor can be as inexpensive as a surface reactor,

216
00:25:02,399 --> 00:25:09,299
then this might have a big and favorable psychological effect.

217
00:25:09,399 --> 00:25:13,460
And after all, one never can have too much safety.

218
00:25:15,359 --> 00:25:19,879
I therefore recommend we go ahead with the reactor.

219
00:25:20,999 --> 00:25:23,940
We consider carefully how best to do it.

220
00:25:25,679 --> 00:25:30,240
We consider all alternatives of other ways

